The equation $\mathbf${v}_i = \frac{d\mathbf{Q}_i}{dt} = $\frac{\hbar}{m_i}$ \text{Im} \left( $\frac{ abla_i \psi}{\psi}$ \right)$defines the velocity \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \mathbf{v}_i \end{tabular} of the $i$-th particle at position \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \mathbf{Q}_i \end{tabular} . This velocity is the rate of change of the particle's position \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}$\frac{d\mathbf{Q}_i}{dt} \end{tabular}$. The velocity is proportional to the imaginary part of the ratio of the gradient of the wave function \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} abla_i \boldsymbol{\psi} \end{tabular} to the wave function itself \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \boldsymbol{\psi} \end{tabular} . The constant \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \boldsymbol{$\hbar} \end{tabular} is the reduced Planck constant, and \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} m_i \end{tabular} is the mass of the particle. The term \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}$\frac{\boldsymbol{ 
abla}_i \boldsymbol{\psi}}{\boldsymbol{\psi}} \end{tabular}$, often referred to as the logarithmic derivative, carries information about the phase and amplitude gradients of the wave function. Specifically, if \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \boldsymbol{\psi}(\mathbf{Q}, t) = R(\mathbf{Q}, t) e^{iS(\mathbf{Q}, t)/\boldsymbol{$\hbar}} \end{tabular}$, where \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} R \end{tabular} is the amplitude and \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} S \end{tabular} is the phase, then \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}$\frac{\boldsymbol{ 
abla} \boldsymbol{\psi}}{\boldsymbol{\psi}} = $\frac{\boldsymbol{ \nabla} R}{R}$ + $\frac{i \boldsymbol{ \nabla} S}{\boldsymbol{\hbar}}$ \end{tabular}$. The imaginary part of this expression is \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}$\frac{\boldsymbol{ 
abla} S}{\boldsymbol{\hbar}} \end{tabular}$. Therefore, the velocity becomes \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \boldsymbol{v}_i = \frac{\boldsymbol{ 
abla} S}{m_i} \end{tabular}$, indicating that the particle's velocity is proportional to the gradient of the phase of its wave function, which dictates the direction of motion. This is analogous to how the direction of propagation of a wave is determined by the wavefronts (surfaces of constant phase).
